<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">2 October in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 2 October, from 48 BCE to 1968.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">48 BCE <span class="tih-ago">2,073 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Julius Caesar arrives in Egypt and learns of Pompey's death</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, pursuing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>, arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, capital of Ptolemaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and learned of his rival's death. Kept there by the Etesian winds, he went on to arbitrate the civil war between the child <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> Ptolemy XIII and Cleopatra.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">829 CE <span class="tih-ago">1,197 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Theophilos succeeds his father Michael II as Byzantine emperor</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">On the death of Michael II, first ruler of the Amorian dynasty, his son Theophilos became Byzantine emperor. He reigned until 842, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, and was the last emperor to support iconoclasm.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophilos-emperor">Theophilos</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/939">939 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">1,087 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Otto I crushes the rebels at the Battle of Andernach</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andernach">Andernach</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, followers of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> decisively defeated rebels led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eberhard-of-franconia">Eberhard of Franconia</a>, the brother of the late King Conrad I. The rebel leaders were killed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> came directly under the crown.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eberhard-of-franconia">Eberhard of Franconia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1187">1187 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">839 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Saladin takes Jerusalem from the Crusaders</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">After a short siege, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> accepted the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, ending 88 years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> rule. Most inhabitants were allowed to ransom themselves, and the city's loss prompted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1263">1263 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">763 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Scots and Norwegians fight the Battle of Largs</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayrshire">Ayrshire</a> coast near Largs, Scots fought part of the storm-battered fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haakon-iv-of-norway">Haakon IV of Norway</a>, who sought to reassert Norwegian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s western seaboard. Haakon withdrew, and in 1266 his successor ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and Man to Scotland.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-largs">Battle of Largs</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1780">1780 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">246 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Major John André is hanged as a British spy</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Major John André, captured carrying Benedict Arnold's plans to surrender West Point, was hanged at Tappan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. His composure won sympathy even among his captors, while Arnold's name became a byword for treason.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1835">1835 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">191 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Texan settlers fire the first shots of the Texas Revolution</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At Gonzales, Texan colonists refused to hand back a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">cannon</a> to Mexican troops and fired on them beneath a flag reading 'Come and Take It'. The skirmish opened the war that led to Texan independence in 1836.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1869">1869 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">157 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Mohandas Gandhi is born in Porbandar</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Mohandas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karamchand-gandhi">Karamchand Gandhi</a> was born in Porbandar, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s western coast. His method of non-violent mass resistance, satyagraha, drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> movement and inspired civil rights campaigns worldwide.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1968">1968 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">58 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Mexican troops open fire on students at Tlatelolco</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Soldiers and police fired on a student rally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plaza-de-las-tres-culturas">Plaza de las Tres Culturas</a>, ten days before the Olympic Games opened. Estimates of the dead range from about 30 to several hundred; the government long concealed the toll.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a></div></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 2 October <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-borromeo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/charles-borromeo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Charles Borromeo</span><span class="tp">1538–1584</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/charles-albert-of-sardinia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/charles-albert-of-sardinia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Charles Albert of Sardinia</span><span class="tp">1798–1849</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/paul-von-hindenburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Paul von Hindenburg</span><span class="tp">1847–1934</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/lal-bahadur-shastri"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Lal Bahadur Shastri</span><span class="tp">1904–1966</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/victor-paz-estenssoro"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/victor-paz-estenssoro.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Víctor Paz Estenssoro</span><span class="tp">1907–2001</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 2 October <span class="n">2</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-iv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pope-urban-iv.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Pope Urban IV</span><span class="tp">c. 1195–1264</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-ix"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/hamengkubuwono-ix.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Hamengkubuwono IX</span><span class="tp">1912–1988</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
